<<preliminaries, echo=FALSE, results='hide'>>=
options(prompt = "R> ", continue = "+  ", width = 70,
  useFancyQuotes = FALSE)
library("panglm")
library("plm")
set.seed(20260824)
@

\author{Imad El Badisy\\Independent Researcher}
\Plainauthor{Imad El Badisy}

\title{\pkg{panglm}: Generalized Linear Models for Panel Data in \proglang{R}}
\Plaintitle{panglm: Generalized Linear Models for Panel Data in R}
\Shorttitle{Panel Generalized Linear Models with \pkg{panglm}}

\Abstract{
  Panel data combine repeated observations with outcome distributions that
  may be continuous, binary, or discrete. Existing software often separates
  linear panel estimators, generalized linear models, high-dimensional fixed
  effects, and mixed models across different interfaces and likelihood
  conventions. The \proglang{R} package \pkg{panglm} provides a unified
  formula interface for pooled, fixed-effects, and random-effects generalized
  linear models with Gaussian, Poisson, binomial, and negative binomial
  responses. Its compiled numerical core implements iteratively reweighted
  least squares, exact conditional likelihoods, absorbed one-way and two-way
  effects, closed-form mixture likelihoods, and Gauss-Hermite quadrature.
  The package supplies model-based and sandwich covariance estimators,
  prediction methods with explicit conditional or marginal semantics,
  diagnostics, hurdle models, and standard model-object methods. This paper
  defines each statistical model, describes the software architecture, and
  demonstrates reproducible comparisons with established implementations.
  The discussion emphasizes where apparently similar panel count models have
  different likelihoods and interpretations.
}

\Keywords{panel data, generalized linear models, fixed effects, random effects,
  conditional likelihood, count data, \proglang{R}}
\Plainkeywords{panel data, generalized linear models, fixed effects, random
  effects, conditional likelihood, count data, R}

