\section[Introduction]{Introduction} \label{sec:introduction}

Panel and longitudinal data arise when units are observed repeatedly. The
resulting dependence can be represented through unit-specific parameters,
latent random effects, or covariance estimators that remain valid under
within-unit correlation. For a Gaussian response, these choices are supported
by mature linear panel software such as \pkg{plm} \citep{Croissant+Millo:2008}.
For non-Gaussian responses, the software landscape is more fragmented.
\pkg{pglm} provides several likelihood-based panel models
\citep{Croissant:2021}; \pkg{fixest} absorbs high-dimensional fixed effects
\citep{Berge:2018}; \pkg{survival} supplies exact conditional logistic
regression \citep{Therneau:2024}; and \pkg{glmmTMB} fits a broad class of
generalized linear mixed models \citep{Brooks+others:2017}.

The \pkg{panglm} package \citep{panglm} brings a defined subset of these
models under one interface. The objective is not to label all estimators as
interchangeable. Instead, the package associates every combination of
\code{model}, \code{family}, and \code{effect} with a specific likelihood or
estimating procedure. This distinction matters particularly for negative
binomial panel models, where conditional, unconditional dummy-variable, and
mixture likelihoods have different parameter interpretations.

The package is implemented in \proglang{R} \citep{R} with a numerical core in
\proglang{C++}, using \pkg{Rcpp} \citep{Eddelbuettel+Francois:2011},
\pkg{RcppArmadillo} \citep{Eddelbuettel+Sanderson:2014}, and
\pkg{RcppParallel} \citep{RcppParallel}. This paper first defines the
supported estimators, then
describes implementation and inference, illustrates the interface, and
summarizes computational validation.

\section{Statistical models} \label{sec:models}

Let $y_{it}$ denote the response for individual $i=1,\ldots,N$ at occasion
$t=1,\ldots,T_i$, and let $x_{it}$ be a $k$-vector of observed covariates. A
general linear predictor is
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:predictor}
  g(\mu_{it}) = x_{it}^\top\beta + \alpha_i + \gamma_t,
\end{equation}
%
where $\alpha_i$ is an individual effect and $\gamma_t$ is an optional time
effect. The treatment of $\alpha_i$ separates pooled, fixed-effects, and
random-effects estimators.

\subsection{Pooled models}

Pooled Gaussian, Poisson, and binomial models omit $\alpha_i$ and $\gamma_t$
from Equation~\ref{eq:predictor}. They are fitted by iteratively reweighted
least squares. The pooled negative binomial model uses the NB2 variance
function
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:nb2}
  \mathrm{Var}(y_{it}\mid x_{it}) = \mu_{it} + \mu_{it}^2/\theta
\end{equation}
%
and alternates coefficient updates with estimation of the positive shape
parameter $\theta$. Pooled point estimates ignore the panel structure, so
cluster-robust covariance estimation is often appropriate.

\subsection{One-way fixed effects}

For Gaussian responses, the individual effect is removed by the within
transformation. With $\bar y_i=T_i^{-1}\sum_t y_{it}$ and
$\bar x_i=T_i^{-1}\sum_t x_{it}$, least squares is applied to
$y_{it}-\bar y_i$ and $x_{it}-\bar x_i$. Time-invariant regressors are not
identified.

For Poisson responses, let $Y_i=\sum_t y_{it}$ and
$L_i(\beta)=\sum_t\exp(x_{it}^\top\beta)$. The individual intercept has the
profile solution
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:poisson-alpha}
  \widehat\alpha_i(\beta)=\log\{Y_i/L_i(\beta)\}.
\end{equation}
%
Substitution yields the conditional Poisson likelihood of
\citet{Hausman+Hall+Griliches:1984}.

For binomial responses, \pkg{panglm} implements the exact conditional
logistic likelihood of \citet{Chamberlain:1980}. If $S_i=\sum_t y_{it}$, the
contribution of stratum $i$ is
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:conditional-logit}
 L_i(\beta\mid S_i)=
 \frac{\exp(\sum_t y_{it}x_{it}^\top\beta)}
 {\sum_{a:\sum_t a_t=S_i}\exp(\sum_t a_tx_{it}^\top\beta)}.
\end{equation}
%
The denominator and inclusion probabilities are computed by a forward and
backward dynamic program. All-zero and all-one strata contain no information
for $\beta$.

The one-way fixed-effects NB2 estimator jointly estimates $\beta$, one
intercept per individual, and $\theta$. This is the unconditional
dummy-variable specification discussed by \citet{Allison+Waterman:2002}, not
the conditional negative binomial model. Explicit intercepts avoid
interpreting the scale restriction of the latter as fixed effects, but they
do not eliminate the general finite-$T$ incidental-parameter concern for a
nonlinear fixed-effects estimator.

\subsection{Two-way fixed effects}

Gaussian two-way effects are removed by alternating projections
\citep{Guimaraes+Portugal:2010}. Poisson and NB2 models use an outer
iteratively reweighted least-squares loop and inner weighted projections.
The algorithm updates $\beta$ using variables partialled out with respect to
individual and time effects, then reconstructs the complete linear predictor.
A general exact two-way conditional logistic likelihood is not available, so
the binomial fixed-effects implementation is one-way.

\subsection{Random effects}

The Gaussian random-effects estimator uses Swamy-Arora feasible generalized
least squares \citep{Swamy+Arora:1972}. The random-effects Poisson model
integrates a mean-one Gamma frailty, producing a closed-form
Poisson-Gamma marginal likelihood. The random-effects negative binomial model
uses a distinct beta-negative-binomial likelihood with two positive shape
parameters. For binomial responses, a Gaussian random intercept is integrated
by Gauss-Hermite quadrature. Response predictions from random-effects models
are marginal over the fitted mixing distribution.

\subsection{Hurdle counts}

The function \code{panglm\_hurdle()} separates the occurrence and intensity
processes for panel counts. The first component is an exact conditional
logistic model for $I(y_{it}>0)$. The second component is a zero-truncated
Poisson or NB2 model for positive observations, with density
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:hurdle}
 f_+(y\mid\vartheta)=\frac{f(y\mid\vartheta)}{1-f(0\mid\vartheta)},
 \qquad y>0.
\end{equation}
%
This follows the standard hurdle decomposition of \citet{Mullahy:1986} while
including individual dummy intercepts in the positive-count component.

\section{Inference and model methods} \label{sec:inference}

Model-based covariance matrices are obtained from the relevant least-squares
system or observed information. For pooled and fixed-effects estimators,
\pkg{panglm} also supplies HC1 and cluster-robust sandwich covariances. With
score contributions $s_j(\widehat\beta)$ and inverse information $B$, the
generic form is
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:sandwich}
 \widehat V(\widehat\beta)=B
 \left\{\sum_j s_j(\widehat\beta)s_j(\widehat\beta)^\top\right\}B.
\end{equation}
%
For conditional logistic regression, the independent likelihood unit is a
complete stratum. Observation components are therefore summed within strata
before the meat of Equation~\ref{eq:sandwich} is formed. A custom cluster may
combine complete strata but cannot split one. Small-sample corrections follow
the HC1 and cluster conventions described by \citet{Cameron+Miller:2015}.

Likelihood metadata count shape, variance, dispersion, and explicit fixed
effects when they belong to the reported likelihood. Conditional Poisson and
conditional logistic likelihoods count only parameters remaining after
conditioning. Consequently, \fct{AIC} and \fct{BIC} are available for
likelihood-based fits but not for Gaussian within or Swamy-Arora estimators.

Objects of class \class{panglm} implement methods for \fct{coef},
\fct{summary}, \fct{vcov}, \fct{confint}, \fct{logLik}, \fct{fitted},
\fct{residuals}, \fct{predict}, and \fct{plot}. Methods for \fct{tidy} and
\fct{glance} support table-building workflows. In-sample values preserve the
row order supplied by the user. Fixed-effects new-data predictions require
known individual and time levels, while exact conditional binomial fits only
provide probabilities conditional on an observed stratum total.

\section{Software design} \label{sec:software}

The primary interface is
\begin{Code}
panglm(formula, data, index, model, family, weights, offset,
  na.action, effect, vcov, R, maxit, tol)
\end{Code}
The \code{index} argument gives the individual and optional time columns.
The formula machinery supports factors and interactions. The model frame is
aligned with the panel indexes, then sorted into contiguous individual blocks
for the compiled routines. Returned fitted values and residuals are mapped
back to input order.

Before fitting, rank is checked after the transformation appropriate to the
estimator. This detects ordinary collinearity as well as regressors removed by
one-way or two-way fixed effects. Unidentified columns are reported and
omitted. Missing responses and covariates follow \code{na.action}; missing or
duplicate panel keys are errors. Nontrivial weights and nonzero offsets are
rejected because weighted conditional likelihoods require statistical models
that are not implemented by the current routines.

The compiled layer parallelizes independent reductions over panel units. It
contains kernels for grouped aggregation, demeaning, likelihood evaluation,
scores, Hessians, and quadrature. Optimization is performed by Newton or
quasi-Newton updates with step control. Scaling is applied internally to
reduce conditioning problems and coefficients are transformed back before
the fitted object is assembled.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{4.1cm}p{3.3cm}@{}}
\hline
Model & Family & Estimator & Reference comparison \\
\hline
Pooled & Gaussian, Poisson, binomial & IRLS & \fct{glm} \\
Pooled & NB2 & Profiled IRLS & \fct{glm.nb} \\
Within & Gaussian & Demeaned least squares & \fct{plm} \\
Within & Poisson & Conditional likelihood & \pkg{pglm}, \pkg{fixest} \\
Within & Binomial & Exact conditional likelihood & \fct{clogit} \\
Within & NB2 & Dummy-variable likelihood & \pkg{fixest} \\
Random & Gaussian & Swamy-Arora FGLS & \fct{plm} \\
Random & Poisson, NB, binomial & Marginal likelihood & \pkg{pglm} \\
Hurdle & Poisson, NB2 & Truncated likelihood & \pkg{pscl} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{\label{tab:estimators} Estimator families and principal reference
implementations used in the package test suite. A listed comparison indicates
agreement only where the statistical likelihoods coincide.}
\end{table}

\section{Illustrations} \label{sec:illustrations}

\subsection{Gaussian investment panel}

The \code{Grunfeld} data contain annual observations for ten firms. Pooled,
one-way fixed-effects, and random-effects models can be fitted with the same
formula.
%
<<grunfeld-fits>>=
data("Grunfeld", package = "plm")
pool_fit <- panglm(inv ~ value + capital, data = Grunfeld,
  index = c("firm", "year"), model = "pooling",
  family = "gaussian")
within_fit <- panglm(inv ~ value + capital, data = Grunfeld,
  index = c("firm", "year"), model = "within",
  family = "gaussian")
random_fit <- panglm(inv ~ value + capital, data = Grunfeld,
  index = c("firm", "year"), model = "random",
  family = "gaussian")
cbind(pooling = coef(pool_fit), random = coef(random_fit))
@
%
The within estimates agree with \pkg{plm} up to numerical precision.
%
<<grunfeld-validation>>=
plm_fit <- plm(inv ~ value + capital, data = Grunfeld,
  index = c("firm", "year"), model = "within")
rbind(panglm = coef(within_fit), plm = coef(plm_fit))
max(abs(coef(within_fit) - coef(plm_fit)))
@
%
The pooled coefficient estimates can be paired with a covariance clustered by
firm without refitting the model.
%
<<grunfeld-inference>>=
sqrt(diag(vcov(pool_fit, type = "cluster")))
confint(pool_fit)
@

\subsection{Panel count regression}

A reproducible Poisson panel is generated from the covariates in
\code{Grunfeld}. The individual fixed-effects estimator conditions on each
firm's total count.
%
<<poisson-example>>=
set.seed(42)
Grunfeld$count <- rpois(nrow(Grunfeld),
  lambda = exp(0.5 + 0.0002 * Grunfeld$value))
poisson_fe <- panglm(count ~ value + capital, data = Grunfeld,
  index = c("firm", "year"), model = "within",
  family = "poisson")
summary(poisson_fe)
@

\subsection{Model visualization}

The plot method displays coefficient intervals for any fitted model and
diagnostic views when fitted values are defined. Figure~\ref{fig:coefficients}
shows the within-estimator coefficients.

\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
<<coefficient-plot, echo=FALSE, fig.height=4.6, fig.width=6.5>>=
par(mar = c(4, 5, 1, 1))
plot(within_fit, which = "coefficients")
@
\caption{\label{fig:coefficients} Coefficient estimates and 95 percent Wald
intervals from the Gaussian fixed-effects model.}
\end{figure}

\section{Validation and scope} \label{sec:validation}

The test suite uses equality comparisons when another implementation fits the
same statistical model. It covers pooled models against \pkg{stats} and
\pkg{MASS} \citep{Venables+Ripley:2002}, Gaussian panel models against
\pkg{plm}, exact conditional logistic regression against \pkg{survival},
absorbed count effects against \pkg{fixest}, compatible marginal likelihoods
against \pkg{pglm}, and truncated counts against \pkg{pscl} \citep{pscl}.
Additional tests
cover sparse and unbalanced panels, boundary groups, rank deficiency,
prediction semantics, model metadata, and original-row-order preservation.

The package intentionally does not provide between estimators, ordinal or
Tobit families, nontrivial observation weights, or nonzero offsets. A two-way
exact conditional logistic estimator is also outside its scope. These
restrictions are checked at the interface so unsupported inputs cannot produce
apparently valid results.

\section{Summary and discussion} \label{sec:summary}

\pkg{panglm} provides one interface for a broad but explicitly defined set of
panel generalized linear models. The implementation combines classical panel
transformations, conditional likelihoods, absorbed effects, mixture models,
and numerical quadrature. Standard model methods expose inference,
information criteria, prediction, diagnostics, and visualization without
hiding differences between conditional and marginal quantities.

Further work could add weighted estimators, offsets, ordinal responses, and
other random-effect distributions. These extensions should enter as new
statistical implementations with their own validation, not as argument
handling layered on an incompatible likelihood.

